---
description: Use exhaustiveCheck so an unhandled pattern-match case is a type error
globs: *.ts,*.tsx
alwaysApply: false
---
- Use `exhaustiveCheck` from [exhaustive-check.ts](mdc:lib/exhaustive-check.ts) at the end of a pattern match. The `never` argument makes an unhandled case a TypeScript error. If that case still runs, it throws. `assertUnreachable` is the same function.
- `exhaustiveCheck(x: never, errorMsg?: string)` throws `errorMsg` when it is passed, and `Unhandled case: ` plus the value when it is omitted.
- Example 1:
  ```ts
  type UserRole = "Admin" | "Manager" | "Employee" | "Guest";
  type Permission = "read-write" | "read" | "denied";
  const checkAccessPermission = (userRole: UserRole, resource: string): Permission => {
    // Admin has access to everything
    if (userRole === "Admin") {
      return "read-write";
    }
    // Manager has access to most resources except system settings
    if (userRole === "Manager") {
      return "read-write";
    }
    // Employee has access to common resources
    if (userRole === "Employee") {
      return "read";
    }
    // Guest has limited access
    if (userRole === "Guest") {
      return "denied";
    }
    return exhaustiveCheck(userRole, `Unhandled user role: ${userRole}`);
  };
  ```
- Example 2:
  ```ts
  type RideType = "Standard" | "Premium" | "Pool";
  const calculateFare = (rideType: RideType, distance: number): number => {
    switch (rideType) {
      case "Standard":
        return distance * 1.5;
      case "Premium":
        return distance * 2.5;
      case "Pool":
        return distance * 1.2;
      default:
        return exhaustiveCheck(rideType);
    }
  };
  ```
